<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Hardened Runtime, which notarization requires, with no exceptions
         asked for. Everything Slab does that sounds like it would need one
         does not:

           • Accessibility (AXUIElement, window tiling) is a TCC grant, not
             an entitlement.
           • Screen Recording (ScreenCaptureKit, `frame`) is likewise TCC.
             SCStreamConfiguration.capturesAudio stays false, so no
             microphone entitlement is needed either.
           • CGEvent.tapCreate and Carbon's RegisterEventHotKey both work
             under default Hardened Runtime. Menu Band proved this on the
             same team ID; see slab/menuband/MenuBand.entitlements.
           • Apple Events are declared in Info.plist with a usage string
             (NSAppleEventsUsageDescription) rather than by entitlement,
             which is what a non-sandboxed app needs.

         Keeping this list empty is the point. Every exception here is a
         promise to Apple and a wider attack surface, and a menu bar app that
         asks for none is one a stranger can reason about. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <false/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <false/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <false/>
</dict>
</plist>
